So now these values look saner than the ones that I initially came up with. The next step is to build the spend share variables and define the relevant time dimensions for the between-season. I did this in R just because the `nflreadr` version of the package lets you automatically merge the play-by-play data. For the play caller data, I am using [Sam Hoppen's data](https://github.com/samhoppen/NFL_public/blob/main/data/all_playcallers.csv). I just take the minimum for each play caller in the dataset and then subtract it from the current season to get their tenure. This approach does have a drawback just because we would expect that there is a bigger jump for a new play caller from the start of their first season to the end of their first season.^[Very much open to suggestions on a better way to do this] Whereas a more experienced play caller it may not actually matter that much by the time they hit year four or five. My "ad spend" is just the share of plays that each play caller makes out of each personnel grouping. 



::: {.cell}

