:::



Then from there we can just bring everything into R. The interesting thing about the Polars parquet writer native parquet writer is that it creates some inconsistencies in the strings. So we do have to resolve them later in R. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true"}
library(ggdist)
library(arrow)
library(MetBrewer)
library(tidyverse)

scheme = schema(
  chain = int64(),
  draw = int64(), 
  obs_id = int64(), 
  x_saturated_pairs = float64(), 
  coach = string(),
  personnel_grouping = string(),
  personnel_contribution = float64(), 
  coach_intercept = float64(),
  overall_mean = float64(),
  season = float64(), 
  week = float64(),
  tenure_relative = float64(), 
  n_explosive = float64(),
  total_plays = int32(),
  time = float64()
)


raw_data = open_dataset('contribution', format =  'parquet', partitioning = c("coach", "personnel_grouping"), schema = scheme) |>
  collect()


make_marginal_lift = raw_data |>
  mutate(
    p_full = plogis(overall_mean),
    p_base = plogis(overall_mean - x_saturated_pairs),
    marginal_lift = p_full - p_base,
    personnel_nice = str_remove(personnel_grouping, 'personnel_'), 
    personnel_nice = as.factor(glue::glue("{personnel_nice} Personnel")), 

  ) |>
    filter(coach %in% c('Kyle Shanahan', 'Sean McVay', 'Andy Reid', 'Sean Payton'))
```
:::



## The Returns on Personnel Groupings

So after an admittedly long-winded section on what I did I think we should try to learn something about football. Instead of focusing on every play caller in the dataset I am going to subset the data to only include Kyle Shanahan, Sean McVay, Andy Reid, and Sean Payton.^[This just means I am dropping Josh McDaniels, Matt LaFleur, Zac Taylor, Kellen Moore, and Arthur Smith] I these four the most interesting due to their reputations and in some ways represent their respective eras of offense/philosophies quite well.


To give you a sense about what these plots are saying on average teams run anywhere between 60-70 plays per game. So if a team has about 60 plays a game and their marginal lift was 0.005 they would have, roughly, 3 explosive plays per game or about an extra explosive every 3 games. At the top I will note we don't see a huge return for any personnel groupings. While I didn't expect huge effects I was still struck by how small the median effects were and how wide the intervals were. I will return to why I suspect this is the case but for now I will just focus on the substance of the findings. 

Kind of unsurprisingly, 11 personnel leads all other personnel groupings in terms of marginal left. Intuitively this makes the most sense. In 11 personnel you have three WRs on the field making it much more amenable for passing and for run concepts if your WRs are good blockers you can leverage that against the defense for explosive runs. A pet cat of analytics for the last 10ish years has been that teams should be passing more because it is more efficient and more effective than passing the ball. 


::: {.cell}

