Initially I was kind of surprised that 21 personnel had the second highest relative lift. This personnel grouping is a hallmark of classic "3 yards and cloud of dust" football or really conservative run heavy football that is meant to grind you down throughout the game. No doubt that some of this is due to the fact that Kyle Shanahan consistently has really explosive offenses and also employs 21 personnel at or near the top of the league. However, this is also structural to how modern offenses work. 

Ben Solak does a much more in depth schematic deep dive on these offenses in this [article](https://www.theringer.com/2024/02/07/nfl/super-bowl-preview-kyle-shanahan-offense-evolution-san-francisco-49ers). To paraphrase Ben pretty heavily how the Shanahan and McVay systems are so effective is how use they use these personnel groupings to move defenders to where they want and then setup the play around where they expect them to be. Take this example from Ben's article. Due to the fact that the Niners are in 21 personnel with Christian McCaffrey and Kyle Juszczyk the Lions are thinking run. The Niners use the combination of motion and keeping Juszczyk and Kittle in to block to free up the middle of the field to hit Deebo. 



<iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/xWGrhk9CJsY?si=_awpnqolNKsfqzZP" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>

We can see a similar thing happening early on in Shanahan's tenure out of 11 personnel. Here Deebo Samuel lines up in the backfield with Matt Breida. If you look at the second level of the defense you see two defensive backs, Nickell Robey-Coleman and Taylor Rapp, lined up alongside a linebacker. This forces the DBs to play out of a spot they are likely not comfortable with. The ideal outcome for the Niners is that Deebo outruns the corner. However, there is also a world where you are forcing the DB to take on a block from an offensive lineman which every coordinator and lineman would take in a heartbeat. 



::: {.cell}

