Part of the story behind this is structural. One of the reasons why the intervals are so tight for Shanahan and McVay is that they run most of their plays out of 21 and 11 personnel respectively. The Rams in particular employ 11 personnel on close to 90% of plays from 2022-2024 according to [Sūmer Sports](https://sumersports.com/teams/offensive/personnel-tendency/?personnel=11). So it makes sense that while they have a lower overall explosive play rate out of these personnel groupings, but the distribution is really tight. In 2023 George Kittle gave an interview with Pardon My Take where he outlines the core of what makes the Shanahan offense tick. 

<iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/OcTPL8l_eUw?si=R5XbRzpVWsAyMvQQ&amp;start=3328" title="Kittle Explains Why The Scheme is So Effective" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>

While you can make the argument that all four play callers are just different flavors of the West Coast offense, Payton in particular tends to mix in more personnel versatility. So much so that in 2019 former Bucs coach Dirk Koetter claimed that the Saints had show [28 different personnel groupings](https://www.nfl.com/news/keys-to-sean-mcvay-s-sean-payton-s-differing-offensive-methods-0ap3000001011448).^[Undoubtedly this is a mixture of how many TEs and RBs are on the field but also who is on the field.] We can see this born out in the model where, for most of, Mcvay's carrer he has primarily employed 12 personnel whereas Payton tends to use a mixture of personnel groupings. 


::: {.cell}

