What stands out wen we compare the distributions of our channel variables is that adspend is pretty variable, whereas our personnel usage is much less variable outside of 11 and 12 personnel. This makes sense since 4 of the 9 play callers are either Sean McVay, Kyle Shanahan, or worked for one or both of them.^[Notably Sean McVay was a TE coach under Kyle Shanhan in 2013 when Kyle Shanahan was offensive coordinator for his dad Mike Shanahan] So their approach to offensive football is going to pretty similar. 

This got me thinking about some posts I get from time to time from one of the founders of Recast on LinkedIn. Recast is an analytics firm that has built out a whole analytics suite to help brands track their return on adspend. Across a few posts he kind of distills why we would likely expect the results we got. The gist of these posts boil down to effectively this graph. Basically, MMM's not only require some level spend across channels but we do also require some noticeable levels of variation. Companies can build this in across ad campaigns by just varying your spend across channels. This gives the model more variation to latch onto a update the posterior accordingly. 

Purposely throttling down usage of one personnel grouping from week-to-week does happen for some coaches, but in all likelihood the extent that it is needed to get any additional signal for each play caller likely doesn't happen. Who is available, who the opponent is, and the game script are going to do a fair amount of heavy lifting for what is going to dictate personnel usage in the game and what gets installed that week. But these changes are going to be pretty meager in comparision to what is needed to get a real signal from the data.
